  { q: 'What does the raven symbolise in Macbeth?', a: 'In Macbeth Act I, scene v, Lady Macbeth says: "The raven himself is hoarse / That croaks the fatal entrance of Duncan / Under my battlements." The raven announces and portends murder — its croak a sign that death is coming to the castle. This draws on the deep folk tradition of the raven as a death omen, a battlefield scavenger whose presence signals bloodshed. Lady Macbeth invokes the raven as the bird of fate at the moment she steels herself to participate in regicide.' },
  { q: 'Where else does Shakespeare use ravens?', a: 'Ravens appear in Othello, Titus Andronicus, As You Like It, 3 Henry VI, and other plays. In Othello (Act IV, scene i), Othello says: "O, it comes o\'er my memory, / As doth the raven o\'er the infected house." In Titus Andronicus (Act II, scene iii), a raven is part of the dark forest imagery associated with rape and murder. In each case, the raven is a signal of impending doom rather than a neutral bird.' },
